    "slug": "djiboutian-peacekeepers-killed-in-clash-with-hawadle-militias-at-beledweyne-airport",
    "summary": "Two Djiboutian peacekeepers were killed and two others wounded in a clash with Hawadle clan militias at Beledweyne's Ugaas Khalif Airport on Thursday. The militias had seized the runway to block a plane carrying federal government troops.",
    "content": "Two Djiboutian soldiers serving with the African Union peacekeeping mission in Somalia were killed and two others wounded on Thursday during a confrontation with Hawadle clan militias at Ugaas Khalif Airport in Beledweyne, central Somalia. The militias, identified as Macawisley fighters, had occupied the runway and surrounding areas to prevent a plane carrying federal government troops from landing, according to local reports and a militia spokesman.\n\nThe clash occurred when the peacekeepers, stationed nearby, tried to push the militias back and secure the airstrip for the incoming aircraft. The plane ultimately aborted its landing. The Hawadle clan has longstanding grievances against the federal government, including disputes over political representation and security arrangements in the Hiiraan region, where Beledweyne is located.\n\nBeledweyne has been a flashpoint for tensions between clan militias and government forces. The Hawadle have previously accused Mogadishu of marginalizing them in national politics and security decisions. The airport seizure appears to be part of ongoing resistance to the deployment of federal troops in the area.\n\nThe African Union mission in Somalia, known as ATMIS, has not commented on the incident. Djiboutian forces are part of ATMIS and are responsible for security in the Hiiraan region. The deaths mark a significant escalation in the confrontation between local militias and peacekeepers.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, the incident highlights the fragility of security in central Somalia and the challenges facing the federal government's efforts to extend its authority. Many diaspora members have family ties to the affected region and closely follow developments. The clash also raises concerns about the effectiveness of international peacekeeping efforts and the potential for renewed conflict.\n\nRead the original report from Somalia Guardian here: https://www.somaliguardian.com/news/somalia-news/somalia-djibouti-peacekeepers-killed-hawadle-militias-beledweyne-airport/",
