    "content": "Somalia's government announced on Thursday that it has given the United Arab Emirates ambassador 72 hours to leave the country and is recalling its own ambassador from Abu Dhabi. The decision was made during a cabinet meeting chaired by Prime Minister Hamza Abdi Barre and was disclosed by Information Minister Abdifatah Kasim Mohamud, who said the UAE had committed violations of Somali sovereignty that officials described as an 'open invasion.' The foreign ministry has been directed to formally notify Emirati ambassador Ahmed Jumaa Mohamed Hathboor of the expulsion order.\n\nThe rupture follows nearly a year of steadily worsening ties between Mogadishu and Abu Dhabi. While the specific incidents behind the latest accusations were not detailed in the announcement, the relationship has been strained by disagreements over political and security matters, including the UAE's role in supporting certain regional administrations and its involvement in port and infrastructure projects. The two countries have also differed on approaches to combating militant groups and managing external partnerships.\n\nThe UAE has been a significant player in Somalia's economic and security landscape, providing training and support to Somali forces and investing in key sectors. However, Mogadishu has grown increasingly wary of what it perceives as Abu Dhabi's unilateral actions that undermine federal authority. The recall of Somalia's envoy and the expulsion of the UAE ambassador signal a major diplomatic escalation and could have ripple effects on bilateral cooperation.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, this development is particularly consequential. Many diaspora members have family ties, business interests, or investments in the UAE, and the country hosts a large Somali expatriate community. A diplomatic break could affect visa and travel arrangements, trade flows, and remittance channels that are vital to families back home. Diaspora-owned businesses that rely on UAE-based logistics and financial services may also face disruptions.\n\nAnalysts suggest the move could reshape regional alliances as Somalia seeks to balance its international partnerships. The government's strong language indicates a willingness to assert sovereignty even at the cost of valuable relationships. How the UAE responds and whether mediation efforts emerge will be closely watched by Somali communities both at home and abroad.\n\nRead the original report from Somalia Guardian here: https://www.somaliguardian.com/news/somalia-news/somalia-orders-uae-ambassador-leave-72-hours-recalls-envoy/",
