    "slug": "poor-roads-and-insecurity-in-baidoa-leave-casual-workers-struggling",
    "summary": "Truck loaders in Baidoa face lost income as damaged roads and landmine fears keep vehicles away, affecting livelihoods and food security for families.",
    "content": "In Baidoa, the capital of Somalia's South West state, casual laborers who load and unload trucks are struggling to support their families as poor road conditions and ongoing insecurity prevent vehicles from reaching the city. Abdimajid Hassan Nur, a father of seven, once earned $5–$6 per day from unloading goods, but now his family relies on one meal a day and credit from local shops.\n\nThe situation has worsened since early October, when heavy rains and the threat of landmines—planted during past conflicts—combined to halt truck traffic into Baidoa. The roads, already damaged, have become impassable in many places, and drivers are unwilling to risk their safety. This has disrupted the supply chain, leaving workers without the daily wages they depend on.\n\nBaidoa has long been a commercial hub for the region, but years of conflict and neglect have left infrastructure fragile. The presence of landmines, a legacy of Somalia's civil war, continues to pose a deadly threat. Local authorities and aid agencies have limited capacity to clear routes or repair roads, exacerbating the economic strain on residents.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, the crisis in Baidoa is a stark reminder of the challenges facing families back home. Many diaspora members send remittances to relatives in the city, and the loss of income for workers like Nur increases the burden on overseas relatives. The diaspora has also invested in local businesses and development projects, which are now at risk as connectivity falters.\n\nThe humanitarian impact is growing, with families cutting back on meals and relying on community support. Without urgent intervention to improve road safety and clearance of landmines, the situation is likely to deteriorate further. International donors and local organizations are being urged to address both the immediate needs of affected families and the long-term infrastructure challenges.\n\nRead the original report from Radio Ergo here: https://radioergo.org/en/2026/11/many-out-of-work-as-poor-roads-and-conflict-cut-off-baidoa-city/",
