    "summary": "The Somali federal government has issued an order to release nearly 150,000 livestock stranded at Bosaso port, where they have been held for weeks due to a dispute over export permits.",
    "content": "The Somali federal government has directed the release of approximately 150,000 livestock that have been stranded at Bosaso port in the Puntland region for several weeks. The animals, which include various types of livestock such as camels, cattle, sheep, and goats, were held at the port due to an ongoing dispute between exporters and local authorities over permits and fees.\n\nThe stranded livestock belong to traders from across Somalia, including many from the diaspora who invest in the livestock export industry. The animals were intended for export to Gulf countries, particularly Saudi Arabia, ahead of the Hajj season when demand for sacrificial animals peaks. However, the dispute led to a halt in exports, causing significant financial losses for traders and disrupting the livelihoods of pastoralists.\n\nThe federal government's intervention comes after mounting pressure from traders and diaspora communities who have invested heavily in the livestock sector. The Minister of Livestock, Forestry, and Range, Hassan Hussein Mohamed, reportedly held talks with Puntland authorities and exporters to resolve the impasse. The exact terms of the agreement have not been disclosed, but the order is expected to allow the livestock to be shipped out in the coming days.\n\nLivestock is a cornerstone of Somalia's economy, contributing about 40% of GDP and employing over 60% of the population. The export of livestock, particularly to Saudi Arabia, is a major source of foreign currency. The Somali diaspora, which sends billions of dollars in remittances annually, also plays a significant role in the sector, with many diaspora-owned businesses involved in trade and logistics. The disruption at Bosaso port has raised concerns about the impact on diaspora investments and the broader economy.\n\nThe federal government's order is seen as a move to ease tensions and restore confidence in the export sector. However, it remains unclear whether the underlying issues, including permit disputes and revenue-sharing between federal and regional authorities, have been fully addressed. Traders have called for a lasting solution to prevent future disruptions.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, the resolution of this crisis is crucial as many have family members dependent on livestock trade and have themselves invested in the sector. The diaspora has also been actively involved in humanitarian efforts, and any prolonged disruption could exacerbate food insecurity in pastoralist communities. The coming weeks will be critical in determining whether the livestock can be exported in time to meet international demand.\n\nRead the original report from Horseed Media here: https://horseedmedia.net/xoolihii-ugu-badnaa-oo-maxjarada-boosaaso-ugu-xayiran-amar-ka-soo-baxay-dfs/427720/",
